WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.730
Modern detection engineering treats detections as living assets.

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:07.989
A detection is proposed, designed, tested, deployed, documented, measured, tuned,

00:00:07.989 --> 00:00:11.479
reviewed, and eventually retired when it no longer provides enough value.

00:00:11.479 --> 00:00:13.698
The lifecycle begins with a reason for the detection.

00:00:13.698 --> 00:00:17.708
That reason may come from threat intelligence, a prior incident, a hunting hypothesis,

00:00:17.708 --> 00:00:21.427
a control requirement, a purple team exercise, or a known visibility gap.

00:00:21.427 --> 00:00:25.347
Design work connects the reason to observable behavior and available telemetry.

00:00:25.347 --> 00:00:28.412
Engineers decide what fields are needed, how the logic should work,

00:00:28.412 --> 00:00:31.766
what context should be included, and how analysts should interpret the result.

00:00:31.766 --> 00:00:34.276
Testing and deployment help prevent avoidable mistakes.

00:00:34.276 --> 00:00:39.145
Teams validate that a detection fires when expected, avoids obvious noise where possible,

00:00:39.145 --> 00:00:41.885
and presents useful information to the people who will use it.

00:00:41.885 --> 00:00:45.835
Maintenance is the part that separates a durable program from a collection of old alerts.

00:00:45.835 --> 00:00:49.464
Environments change, log schemas change, business processes change,

00:00:49.464 --> 00:00:50.944
and threat patterns change.

00:00:50.944 --> 00:00:53.960
Mature teams review detections as part of normal operations.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.383
Lifecycle management also clarifies ownership.

00:00:56.383 --> 00:00:59.643
Someone should know why a detection exists, which data it depends on,

00:00:59.643 --> 00:01:02.293
how it was validated, what response guidance applies,

00:01:02.293 --> 00:01:03.642
and when it should be reviewed again.

00:01:03.642 --> 00:01:05.412
Retirement is part of the lifecycle too.

00:01:05.412 --> 00:01:09.117
If a detection no longer maps to relevant systems, creates recurring noise,

00:01:09.117 --> 00:01:10.827
or depends on unavailable data,

00:01:10.827 --> 00:01:14.521
retiring or redesigning it may be healthier than keeping it for appearance.

00:01:14.521 --> 00:01:17.251
A healthy lifecycle creates evidence as well as alerts.

00:01:17.251 --> 00:01:19.626
Design notes, test results, review decisions,

00:01:19.626 --> 00:01:24.470
and tuning history help teams understand how the detection changed and why those changes

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:24.480
were made.
